**Dual-write (phase 1).** Legacy `pg-orders-1` stays the source of truth: every read and write goes to it. Each write also commits an outbox row in the same transaction, and `outbox-relay` applies those rows to orders-v2 asynchronously, while `backfill_orders_v2` copies historical orders each night over about 11 nights. Nothing reads from orders-v2 yet, so its errors can't reach customers. Phase 1 passes only when four things hold:

- the backfill is complete;
- the `order-diff` mismatch rate stays under 0.01% for 3 straight days;
- the relay's p99 lag stays under 2 seconds for those same 3 days;
- a one-time full-table checksum matches, which is the only check that covers every old order.

**Cutover (phase 2).** The `orders.read_from_v2` flag moves reads to orders-v2 in steps of 1%, 10%, 50% and 100%, one step a day, while writes still go to both stores. Reads then stay at 100% on orders-v2 for a 48-hour soak. After that, `orders.write_legacy` is turned off, orders-v2 becomes the only store receiving writes, and the relay is scaled to zero. Legacy stays up read-only for 30 days and is then decommissioned under `CHG-4471`. The cutover counts as done after 7 days with no `ORD-4xx` incident and order-history p99 at or under 180 ms.

The main caveat is that rollback only works up to the write stop. Until then, setting `orders.read_from_v2` back to 0 reverts reads within a minute with no data loss. Once `orders.write_legacy` is off, nothing syncs back to legacy, so falling back would lose every order written after that point. That is why the write stop waits for the soak and needs sign-off from the DBA lead and the commerce director. It is also tied to the calendar: if it misses 2026-11-30, the code freeze pushes it to January, and dual-write keeps running until then.
